olumn 11 has 3 (y=6), 7 (y=9), 4 (y=14), 5 (y=16): rows 0–6 → 3, 7–9 → 7, 10–14 → 4, 15–16 → 5. The two-cell objects elsewhere (the 4s at (1,6),(1,7), the 1s in column 10) are not in marked columns and stay as they are.\n\n**Construction stages.** I emit one canvas per marked column, scanning the column from the bottom up and painting each background cell with the colour of the last seed seen below it.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    h, w = g.shape\n    marked = [x for x in range(w) if g[h - 1, x] == 5]\n    for x in marked:\n        cur = None\n        for y in range(h - 1, -1, -1):\n            if g[y, x] != bg:\n                cur = g[y, x]\n            elif cur is not None:\n                out[y, x] = cur\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"column {x} (marked by a 5 on the bottom row): each empty cell takes the colour of the nearest seed below it\")\n    emit(out.copy(), \"all marked columns filled; other columns unchanged\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"pty cell takes the colour of the nearest coloured cell *below* it in the same column — the colours \"flood upward\" from their seeds until they hit the next seed above."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"14aa8bf83b0a33ae6fb06d5aa6bd140f78dd8a0c52089a8aedd038c6379b3afa","task_sha256":"315333e753913c8ed702fb7f7f7a32424d4e6385c0a6f0c17263fc92e7494383","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
